OBSERVATIONS (not failures against criteria 1-5; lead adjudicates)
  a) Spec W1 req 1 says rows without SubItems must be "byte-identical in
     output". They are not literally byte-identical: the `{{if}}/{{range}}`
     block leaves one whitespace-only line (12 spaces + newline) per breakdown
     row. Measured by rendering a 3-row no-SubItems fixture against the HEAD
     template and against the working template and diffing:
       27a28 / 34a36 / 40a43, each added line is `            ` and nothing else
       (cat -A shows "            <EOL>").
     Whitespace-only; no visible or semantic change. The oracle does not test
     byte-identity, and the spec designates the oracle as the authoritative
     acceptance statement for attempt 3.
  b) Degenerate tiny-base case: base $0.01 with a x0.5 phase renders
     total $0.01, base $0.01, adjustment "+$0.00" -- a "+" on a shrinking
     phase. The displayed identity still holds (1 + 0 == 1); this is the
     unavoidable consequence of cent-exact display rounding, not a defect in
     the fix. Flagging because it is the one case where the sign glyph
     contradicts the phase label.
  c) The template-level fractional-cent test compares float64s (see criterion 3)
     rather than integer cents. Cannot produce a false PASS at these
     magnitudes, but it is weaker than the oracle it mirrors.

